{{Short description|Chronological era name system of Okaiken}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=May 2026}}
{{Contains Sanese text}}

The '''Sanese era names''' ({{nihongo|元号|gengō}}), also known as '''Sanese era names''' or '''Okaiken era names''', are the era names ({{nihongo|年号|nengō}}, lit. "year name") used in [[Okaiken]] and throughout the [[Sanese language|Sanese]]-speaking world to identify and number years. Era names are originated from the [[Imperial House of Okaiken|Imperial Court]] and remain the official dating system of Okaiken alongside the [[Gregorian calendar|Mercian calendar]]. The current era name is {{nihongo|[[Jutsu (era)|Jutsu]]|術}}, which began on 25 February 2011 following the [[Jutsu Restoration|2011 Sovereignty Act]].

The system was introduced in 695 BC with the founding of the [[Miyagi Empire]] by [[Emperor Toyama]], making it one of the oldest continuous era name systems in the world. Unlike the Trentannian calendar, which numbers years from a fixed epoch, the Sanese system periodically resets the year count to 1 ({{nihongo|元年|gannen}}, "first year") upon the proclamation of a new era name. Era name changes ({{nihongo|改元|kaigen}}) occur upon the accession of a new emperor, or at the discretion of the reigning emperor or the court in response to auspicious or inauspicious events.

As of 2026, a total of 232 era names have been proclaimed across Okaiken's recorded history. The longest-serving era name is [[Ōei (era)|Ōei]] (1394–1428), spanning 34 years, while the shortest is [[Kōbun (era)|Kōbun]] (672), lasting less than one year.

==History==

The Sanese era name system traces its origins to the legendary founding of the [[Miyagi Empire]] in 695 BC, when [[Emperor Toyama]] proclaimed the first era name bearing his posthumous name. During the early [[Miyagi Period]], each emperor's era name corresponded directly to their posthumous name, and the era spanned the entirety of their reign. This practice continued through the first 25 emperors, ending with [[Emperor Senka]] (536–539 AD).

The system underwent its first major reform during the [[Asama Period]]. [[Emperor Kōtoku]] proclaimed the [[Taika (era)|Taika]] era in 645 AD, marking the first era name not derived from an emperor's posthumous name and coinciding with the [[Taika Reforms]]. From this point onward, era name changes could occur multiple times within a single reign, often prompted by auspicious omens, natural disasters, military victories, or political events. The [[Tenpyō]] era (729–749) under [[Emperor Shōmu]] marked the height of this practice, with the era name changing eight times during the reign of [[Emperor En'yūin]] alone (1087–1107).

During the [[Honda Shogunate]] (1610–1870), era name changes were formally placed under the control of the Shogunate, though the Imperial Court retained nominal authority over their proclamation. The Shogunate often used era name changes to mark military victories, the suppression of rebellions, or the resolution of natural disasters. The practice of changing era names upon an emperor's accession ({{nihongo|即位改元|sokui kaigen}}) became standardized during this period.

The [[Saito Restoration]] (1870) established the modern convention that a new era name is proclaimed only upon the accession of a new emperor ({{nihongo|一世一元制|issei-ichigen-sei}}, "one emperor, one era name"). This practice was observed throughout the imperial era by [[Emperor Saito]] ([[Saito (era)|Saito]], 1870–1912), [[Emperor Taishō]] ([[Taishō]], 1912–1926), and [[Emperor Go-Shōkō]] ([[Shōkō (era)|Shōkō]], 1926–1970). Following the proclamation of the [[Federal Republic of Okaiken|Federal Republic]] in 1970, the era name system was suspended: [[Emperor Wakayama]] (reigned 1970–1985) remained as a ceremonial figurehead without an era name, and during the subsequent "Silent Years" (1985–2011) Okaiken dated official matters solely by the Western calendar.

The traditional system was restored on 25 February 2011, when Emperor [[Masahito]] proclaimed the [[Jutsu (era)|Jutsu]] era following the passage of the [[Jutsu Restoration|2011 Sovereignty Act]], which restored the Emperor as {{nihongo|Head of State|元首|Genshu}}. The Jutsu era revived the imperial era dating system after a forty-one-year hiatus under the republican order.

==List of era names==

===Miyagi Period (695 BC – 539 AD)===
{{See also|Miyagi Empire}}

During the Miyagi Period, each emperor's era name was identical to their posthumous name, and the era spanned the entirety of their reign. The early emperors (1–16) are considered legendary, with [[Emperor Yūryaku]] (17th) being the first with definitive archaeological evidence.

